<translate>
<!--T:9-->
This page provides an overview of '''copyright rules of Ukraine''' relevant to uploading works into Wikimedia Commons.
Note that any work originating in {{wp-Ukraine|Ukraine}} must be in the public domain, or available under a free license, in both Ukraine and the United States before it can be uploaded to Wikimedia Commons. If there is any doubt about the copyright status of a work from Ukraine, refer to the relevant laws for clarification.

==Background== <!--T:10-->

<!--T:11-->
In the past, Ukraine was controlled at different times in whole or part by Kievan Rus', Lithuania, Poland, Russia and Austria.
After World War I, Ukraine became a founding member of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics in December 1922.
During the dissolution of the USSR, on 24 August 1991 the Ukrainian parliament adopted the Act of Independence.

<!--T:12-->
Ukraine has been a member of the {{wp-Berne Convention|Berne Convention}} since 25 October 1995, the {{wp-WIPO Copyright Treaty|WIPO Copyright Treaty}} since 6 March 2002 and the {{wp-World Trade Organization|World Trade Organization}} since 16 May 2008.</translate><ref name=Ukraine-WIPO/>
<translate>

<!--T:13-->
As of 2023 the {{wp-World Intellectual Property Organization|World Intellectual Property Organization}} (WIPO), an agency of the United Nations, listed "''Law of Ukraine No. 2811-IX of December 1, 2022, on Copyright and Related Rights''" as the main copyright law enacted by the legislature of Ukraine.<ref name=Ukraine-WIPO/>
WIPO holds the text of this law in their {{wp-WIPO Lex|WIPO Lex}} database.<ref name=Law2023/> The law repeals the "''Law of Ukraine No. 3792-XII of December 23, 1993, on Copyright and Related Rights (as amended up to October 27, 2016)''" and its successor "''Law No. 3792-XII of December 23, 1993, on Copyright and Related Rights (as amended up to April 26, 2017)''", both of which are also available in the WIPO database.<ref name=Law2016/><ref name=Law2017/></translate>
<translate>

=== Historical copyright terms in the Ukrainian SSR === <!--T:56-->

<!--T:57-->
Before the adoption of the Ukrainian copyright law of 1993, works originating in the Ukrainian SSR were subject to Soviet Union and Ukrainian SSR copyright legislation. Copyright terms changed several times during the Soviet period:

<!--T:58-->
The USSR [[<tvar name="1">s:ru:Постановление ЦИК и СНК СССР от 30.01.1925 «Об основах авторского права»</tvar>|Fundamentals of Copyright of 30 January 1925]] generally protected a work for '''25 years from its first publication'''.
The USSR [[<tvar name="2">s:ru:Постановление ЦИК и СНК СССР от 16.05.1928 основы авторского права</tvar>|Fundamentals of Copyright of 16 May 1928]] replaced this with protection for the '''life of the author and 15 years after death'''. This rule was implemented in the Ukrainian SSR by the [[<tvar name="3">s:uk:ЗЗРРСУУ/1929/1/7/Про авторське право</tvar>|Resolution on Copyright of 6 February 1929]]. Article 15 provided that copyright passed to the author's heirs for 15 years, calculated from 1 January of the year of the author's death. The 1929 law also provided shorter terms for certain categories: individual photographic works were protected for 5 years and collections of photographs for 10 years.
The [[<tvar name="4">s:uk:Цивільний кодекс УРСР 1963 року</tvar>|Civil Code of the Ukrainian SSR of 1963]], which entered into force on 1 January 1964, retained the general rule of '''life plus 15 years''' (Articles 493–494). It also retained the special terms of 5 years for individual photographic works and 10 years for collections of photographs.
Following the USSR's adherence to the Universal Copyright Convention, the term was extended to '''life plus 25 years''' with effect from 1 June 1973. In the Ukrainian SSR this was implemented by the [[s:uk:Про внесення змін і доповнень до Цивільного кодексу Української РСР|Decree of 13 September 1974 amending the Civil Code of the Ukrainian SSR]]. The Decree expressly stated that the new 25-year term did '''not''' apply to works whose copyright had already expired before 1 January 1973.

<!--T:59-->
These historical terms may therefore be relevant when determining whether copyright in an older work had already expired before later extensions of the term of protection.

==General rules== <!--T:14-->

<!--T:15-->
Under ''Law No. 3792-XII of December 23, 1993 as amended up to April 26, 2017)'',
*Copyright in a work arises from the fact of its creation and takes effect from the date of creation.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(1)}}
*Copyright is valid throughout the life of the author and for 70 years after his death, except as provided below.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(2)}}
</translate>
<translate>
<!--T:16-->
*For works published anonymously or under a pseudonym, copyright expires 70 years after the work was released. If the pseudonym adopted by the author leaves no doubt as to the identity of the author or the author is revealed no later than 70 years after the publication of the work, copyright lasts for the author's life plus 70 years.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(3)}}
*Copyright in collaborative works is valid for life and 70 years after the death of the last co-author.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(4)}}
</translate>
<translate>
<!--T:17-->
*Copyright in works of posthumously rehabilitated authors is valid for 70 years after their rehabilitation.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(6)}} Unless author has passed away before January 1th 1951.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 section VI-4}}
*Copyright in a work first published within 30 years after the author's death is valid for 70 years from the date of its lawful publication.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(7)}}
*Persons who first publish an unpublished work after its copyright protection expires enjoy protection for 25 years from the time when the work was first published.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(8)}}
*Calculation of the terms of copyright starts from 1 January of the year following the relevant event.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Art.28(9)}}

<!--T:18-->
Copyright protection under the 1993 act applied to all works where the copyright had not expired before the act became effective.{{sref|3792-XII/199-2017 Final.4}}
A Ukrainian or Ukrainian SSR work is in the public domain in Ukraine if it was published before 1 January 1951, and the creator (if known) died before that date. This is the effect of the copyright term extension from 50 to 70 years in 2001.

<!--T:19-->
A Ukrainian or Ukrainian SSR work that is in the public domain in Ukraine according to this rule is in the public domain in the U.S. only if it was in the public domain in Ukraine before 1 January 1996, e.g. if it was published before 1 January 1946 and the creator died before this date, and no copyright was registered in the U.S.
This is the combined effect of the 1993 law, Ukraine's joining the {{wp-Berne Convention for the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works|Berne Convention}} in 1996, and of 17 USC 104A with its critical date of January 1, 1996.</translate><ref name=USGov104a/>
<translate>
